  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 228 AM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nThe central US continues to remain under the influence of the large \nupper level cyclone situated across the desert southwest. Further \nnorth, the main jet stream connects two upper lows; one across the \nPNW and the other approaching the Hudson Bay. Back across the \ncentral Plains, mid and upper level moisture continues to stream \nnortheast ahead of the main trough axis as compact upper-level \nridging keeps the region warm. With WAA within this pattern expected \nto continue today, high temperatures will again creep back into the \nupper 70s and low 80s as broken decks of mid and high clouds stream \nin across Kansas. By late this afternoon and evening, the surface \ncyclone and inverted trough deepens across central Kansas in \nresponse to the eastward progression of the upper cyclone. This \nshould shift a good focal point of low-level isentropic ascent and \nmid-level ascent across central and north-central Kansas. Rain \nchances will be expected within a narrow corridor into Monday \nmorning across central and north-central KS with chances quickly \nwaning as one heads east of Hwy 81. Most areas stay dry Monday with \nthe exception of areas near the Hwy 81 corridor. Scattered \nprecipitation chances (20-30%) do not seem to shift into eastern \nKansas until Tuesday morning as the upper-level cyclone finally \nejects off the central Rockies and across Nebraska. A weak cold \nfront slides across the area Tuesday yielding low-end PoPs (20-40%) \nand slightly cooler afternoon temps (low to mid 70s).\n\nBy Wednesday and the remainder of the week, temperatures are \nexpected to rebound back into the upper 70s and low 80s as \nsouthwesterly flow sets up across much of the western and central \nCONUS. Several nodes of vorticity embedded in the flow will move \nacross the region keeping low-end PoPs in the forecast for the \nremainder of October. That said, not expecting much in terms of \ncopious amounts of rainfall.",
